[{"order":1,"type":"heading","text":"Assertions and Web-First Assertions in Playwright"},{"order":2,"type":"paragraph","text":"Writing robust end-to-end tests requires reliable assertions. Playwright introduces the concept of Web-First Assertions, which automatically retry until the expected condition is met or the timeout is reached. This drastically reduces flaky tests caused by timing issues and asynchronous UI updates."},{"order":3,"type":"subheading","text":"Understanding Standard vs. Web-First Assertions"},{"order":4,"type":"paragraph","text":"Standard testing libraries use synchronous assertions like expect(value).toBe(true). While useful for static data, they fail when applied to dynamic web elements because they do not wait for the element to appear or change state. Playwright's web-first assertions use the expect(locator).toBeVisible() syntax, which handles retrying automatically."},{"order":5,"type":"subheading","text":"Common Web-First Assertions"},{"order":6,"type":"bullet_point","text":"expect(locator).toBeVisible(): Ensures the element is present in the DOM and visible on the page."},{"order":7,"type":"bullet_point","text":"expect(locator).toHaveText(): Verifies that the element matches the expected text string or regular expression."},{"order":8,"type":"bullet_point","text":"expect(locator).toBeEnabled(): Confirms that a button or input field is enabled for user interaction."},{"order":9,"type":"bullet_point","text":"expect(locator).isChecked(): Validates that a checkbox or radio button is checked."},{"order":10,"type":"subheading","text":"Code Example: Using Web-First Assertions"},{"order":11,"type":"code","text":"const { test, expect } = require('@playwright/test');

test('verify user login flow', async ({ page }) => {
  await page.goto('https://example.com/login');

  // Fill in credentials
  await page.locator('#username').fill('test_user');
  await page.locator('#password').fill('secure_password');

  // Click login button
  await page.locator('button[type="submit"]').click();

  // Web-first assertion with automatic retry
  const welcomeMessage = page.locator('.welcome-banner');
  await expect(welcomeMessage).toBeVisible();
  await expect(welcomeMessage).toHaveText('Welcome, test_user!');
});"},{"order":12,"type":"subheading","text":"Configuring Timeouts for Assertions"},{"order":13,"type":"paragraph","text":"By default, web-first assertions wait up to 5,000 milliseconds (5 seconds) before timing out. You can override this timeout for a specific assertion using the timeout option if you are dealing with slower network requests or animations."},{"order":14,"type":"code","text":"await expect(page.locator('.slow-loading-element')).toBeVisible({ timeout: 10000 });"},{"order":15,"type":"subheading","text":"Negative Assertions"},{"order":16,"type":"paragraph","text":"You can also assert that a condition is NOT met by chaining the not modifier. Playwright will retry until the element successfully disappears or loses the specified property."},{"order":17,"type":"code","text":"// Ensure the loading spinner disappears
await expect(page.locator('.spinner')).not.toBeVisible();"},{"order":18,"type":"subheading","text":"Conclusion"},{"order":19,"type":"paragraph","text":"Web-first assertions are a cornerstone of reliable Playwright test suites. By leveraging built-in auto-waiting and retry mechanisms, you can eliminate arbitrary pauses (like sleep statements) and write clean, resilient, and fast automated tests."}]
